sure it might just be a prologue but  Metal Gear Solid 5 Ground zeroes is an  important milestone for the stealth  genre it has taken the series to an  open-world playground and redefined how  stealth works within that largest scope  and in doing so they found a way to make  failure fun little fairy isn't a  self-camera  always just around the next corner you  know don't get spotted don't make noise  moving through the world you're always  balancing on the knife's edge  that's why stealth is tense but some  find it too punishing  I can understand that you know it's  frustrating when a stealth game throws  you into a game over screen if the enemy  so much as sees your foot sticking out  from behind a crate so recent stealth  games have started focusing on how to  approach failure design that means  they're trying to make themselves feel  less abusive to you when you mock up now  they do this by replacing reasons to  simply load a savefile with gameplay  mechanics that allow you to respond to  failure in new and interesting ways  you've probably seen some recent stealth  games making a big fuss over how they  now just kind of let you shoot everyone  that's one way of addressing failure  design by making combat controls tight  and accessible Splinter Cell blacklist  did it do  hitman absolution did it  even the new thief tried to better  support combat but why would you want to  sneak your way through if it's just as  easy to pull out a gun and shoot your  way through Ground zeroes has the answer  by giving you the chance to turn every  little mistake into an opportunity the  sheer scope of ground zeroes open world  creates this vast playpen of  interconnected systems that exist to  nudge you closer to failure getting  spotted or creating a loud enough  disturbance completely disrupts the  behavior of enemies across the entirety  of camp Omega the sense that you're  actually infiltrating a large location  is all the more real when that location  exists without load screens and when the  consequences for disturbing the enemies  within that space are felt across its  entirety because this larger location  means that you've got way more chances  to mess up ground zeros removes the  series iconic radar and replaces it with  the ability to spot enemies and track  their locations through walls the  failure of alerting enemies all making  them suspicious becomes fun because the  work you put into marking those enemies  allows you to poke and prod at the enemy  AI with intent want to get through a  heavily guarded area no problem  plant some c4 charges on the opposite  side of the base  sneak back to the guarded area detonate  the explosives and watch the markers  above all the enemies you've spotted  leave their posts to investigate giving  you the opportunity to slip by but what  if one of those guards catches sight of  you as he's running to investigate the  explosion  ground zeroes new reflex system is  designed to account for this small  self-contained failure the game goes  into slow motion and you've got a few  seconds to take the gun out before he  radios in and alerts the entire base  it's a fantastic piece of failure design  because it takes the games polished  action controls and gives you the  opportunity to correct the situation  with a bullet to their face but it's not  a free kill you still have to deal with  the consequences like the sound of  gunfire or having to hide the body and  what's fascinating about ground zeroes  is how its open-world gives these  consequence  is the scope to spread out like a  butterfly effect check out this example  here I've been tasked with killing two  specific enemies while trying to sneak  up on the first I get spotted so I have  to take him down quickly it doesn't go  so well he goes down but the enemy knows  I'm here with camper Omega alerted I  know that the second target will try to  flee which will actually fail the  mission so in a panic I sprint to the  nearest armored vehicle when who do I  see but my second target trying to drive  off energy right next to me  not only can I easily shoot him down but  now I've got the perfect mode of  transport to escape the hail of gunfire  that's about to descend on my location  small failures in ground zeroes can  snowball into something worse but you  always have the chance to deal with the  situation and even turn failure into  opportunity and that is the heart of  good failure design
 

